# Contributing

Thanks for contributing to `[[ project_name ]]`!

## Pre-commit hooks

Each commit is checked by [pre-commit](https://pre-commit.com/) hooks defined in
[`.pre-commit-config.yaml`](.pre-commit-config.yaml):

- **[Betterleaks](https://github.com/betterleaks/betterleaks)** scans the staged
  changes for secrets (API keys, tokens, passwords).
- **[Semgrep](https://semgrep.dev/)** scans the staged Python, YAML, and
  Dockerfile changes for security issues (SAST).
[%- if platform == 'github' %]
- **[actionlint](https://github.com/rhysd/actionlint)** checks GitHub Actions
  workflows for errors.
- **[zizmor](https://docs.zizmor.sh/)** audits GitHub Actions workflows,
  `dependabot.yml`, and `.pre-commit-config.yaml` for security issues. Set
  `GH_TOKEN` (e.g. `GH_TOKEN=$(gh auth token)`) to add its online audits; CI
  always runs them.
[%- else %]
- **[check-jsonschema](https://check-jsonschema.readthedocs.io/)** checks
  `azure-pipelines.yml` against the Azure Pipelines schema, so a misspelled
  key fails the commit instead of the pipeline run.
[%- endif %]

The hooks run from [`.githooks/pre-commit`](.githooks/pre-commit), which works
both inside the dev container and directly on your machine. The dev container
enables it for you. Otherwise, enable it once per clone with:

```
git config core.hooksPath .githooks
```

The only requirement is [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) (or `pre-commit`)
on your PATH. If you use the dev container, the setting is shared with your
machine, so commits from a host terminal, a desktop git client, or an IDE outside the
container are checked as well. Don't use `pre-commit install`: the hook it
generates is tied to one Python environment, so it breaks commits made from
the other side of the container. Setting `core.hooksPath` also means git stops
running any hooks of your own in `.git/hooks`; move them into `.githooks` (or
call them from it) if you want to keep them.

The first commit afterwards takes a minute while pre-commit builds the tools;
later commits take a few seconds. To run every hook against the whole tree:

```
uv run pre-commit run --all-files
```

CI runs the same hooks on every pull request, so a commit made with
`--no-verify` is still checked. CI also runs two scans that are not hooks:
Betterleaks over the **full git history**, and
[Trivy](https://trivy.dev/) over `uv.lock` (known-vulnerable dependencies) and
the dev container Dockerfile (misconfigurations). Trivy's settings are in
[`trivy.yaml`](trivy.yaml); to run it locally,
[install Trivy](https://trivy.dev/latest/getting-started/installation/) and run
`trivy fs .` from the repo root. On a pull request, Trivy fails only on
findings the base branch doesn't already have; the rest show as warnings.

[%- if platform == 'github' %]

Trivy and Semgrep also run weekly and on every push to `main`
([Security](.github/workflows/security.yml) workflow), because new CVEs and
updated Semgrep rules can turn up findings without any code change. These runs
fail on every finding, so a new advisory turns `main` red instead of blocking
unrelated pull requests. Their
findings are listed under the repository's **Security > Code scanning** tab,
and a failing run opens a "Security scan failing on main" issue.
[%- else %]

The whole pipeline ([`azure-pipelines.yml`](azure-pipelines.yml)) also runs
weekly and on every push to `main`, because new CVEs and updated Semgrep rules
can turn up findings without any code change. These runs fail on every
finding, so a new advisory turns `main` red instead of blocking unrelated pull
requests. Findings are in the failed job's log. Azure DevOps doesn't notify
anyone of a failing scheduled run by default; subscribe to "A build fails"
under **Project settings > Notifications**.
[%- endif %]

### False positives

Confirm the finding really is harmless, then suppress it as narrowly as
possible, with a comment saying why:

| Tool        | Suppress with                                                                        |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Betterleaks | add the finding's fingerprint to [`.betterleaksignore`](.betterleaksignore)          |
| Semgrep     | a `# nosemgrep: <rule-id>` comment on the flagged line                               |
[%- if platform == 'github' %]
| zizmor      | a `# zizmor: ignore[<audit>]` comment on the flagged line                            |
[%- endif %]
| Trivy       | add the vulnerability ID to a `.trivyignore` file (prefer upgrading the package)     |

## Claude Code

[`.claude/settings.json`](.claude/settings.json) is shared project settings
for [Claude Code](https://code.claude.com/docs):

- Error reporting is off. Telemetry stays on: turning it off also stops
  Claude Code fetching feature flags, which disables features such as
  Remote Control and artifact comments.
- No Claude attribution (`Co-Authored-By`, "Generated with Claude Code") in
  commits or pull requests.
- Claude can't read env files (`.env`, `.env.*`, `*.env`, `.envrc`) or
  `secrets/`; `.env.example`, the committed list of variables, stays
  readable. This covers Claude's file tools and simple shell reads like
  `cat .env`, not a program Claude runs that opens the file itself.
- Claude can't read credential files in your home folder: SSH keys, the
  GitHub CLI token, AWS/Google Cloud/Azure credentials, `.netrc`, `.pypirc`,
  and Docker's `config.json`.
- Claude always asks before committing, pushing, creating, merging, or
  closing a pull request, creating or deleting a release or repository, and
  before git commands that discard work (`reset --hard`, `clean`, `checkout`,
  `restore`, `branch -D`, `stash drop`/`clear`, `rebase`), even in auto mode.
- Claude doesn't ask before reading: file reads and searches (outside the
  denied files above), read-only git commands (`status`, `diff`, `log`,
  `show`, `fetch`, ...), and viewing pull requests and CI runs with
[% if platform == 'github' %]  `gh` or the GitHub MCP server.
[% else %]  `az repos pr` and `az pipelines runs`.
[% endif -%]
- The `security-guidance` plugin is enabled. The dev container installs it;
  elsewhere, install it once with
  `claude plugin install security-guidance@claude-plugins-official --scope project`.

These guard against Claude being over-eager, not against deliberate misuse:
a program Claude runs can still read files, and
[% if platform == 'github' %]`gh api`[% else %]`az devops invoke`[% endif %] isn't restricted.

Personal overrides go in `.claude/settings.local.json` (git-ignored). Its
permission rules add to the deny and ask rules above but can't lift them;
its other settings (`env`, `attribution`, `enabledPlugins`) override these.

## Pull request titles

This repository **squash-merges** pull requests, so the **PR title becomes the
commit subject on `main`**. PR titles must follow
[Conventional Commits 1.0.0](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/)
[%- if platform == 'github' %] and
are validated automatically by the [PR Title](.github/workflows/pr-title.yml)
workflow.
[%- else %].
Azure DevOps doesn't check them, so reviewers do. When completing a pull
request, Azure DevOps suggests "Merged PR <n>: <title>" as the commit
message; delete the "Merged PR <n>: " prefix so the subject is the title.
[%- endif %] Individual commit messages within a PR are collapsed at squash and are
not policed — only the title matters.

The format is:

```
<type>[optional scope]: <description>
```

Examples:

```
feat: add export command
fix(parser): handle empty input
docs: document configuration options
build(deps): bump httpx to 0.28.1
```

### Allowed types

| Type       | Use for                                                        |
| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `feat`     | A new feature                                                  |
| `fix`      | A bug fix                                                      |
| `docs`     | Documentation only changes                                     |
| `style`    | Formatting, whitespace — no code-behavior change               |
| `refactor` | A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature      |
| `perf`     | A change that improves performance                             |
| `test`     | Adding or correcting tests                                     |
| `build`    | Changes to the build system or dependencies                   |
| `ci`       | Changes to CI configuration and scripts                        |
| `chore`    | Other changes that don't modify `src` or `test` files         |
| `revert`   | Reverts a previous commit                                      |

A breaking change is marked with a `!` after the type/scope (e.g. `feat!:`) or a
`BREAKING CHANGE:` footer.
[%- if platform == 'github' %]

### How titles drive releases

Releases are automated by
[release-please](https://github.com/googleapis/release-please) (see
[`release.yml`](.github/workflows/release.yml)), so the PR title also decides
the next version and the changelog entry:

| Title                                                              | Version bump | Changelog section        |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------ | ------------------------ |
| `feat!:` / `BREAKING CHANGE:`                                      | major        | ⚠ BREAKING CHANGES       |
| `feat:`                                                            | minor        | Features                 |
| `fix:`                                                             | patch        | Bug Fixes                |
| `perf:`                                                            | patch        | Performance Improvements |
| `revert:`                                                          | patch        | Reverts                  |
| `build:`, `chore:`, `ci:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `style:`, `test:` | none         | not listed               |

The last row's types never trigger a release on their own. If a release is
already pending, they're included in it but left out of the changelog. That
includes `docs:`: documentation changes ship with the next code release (set
in `changelog-sections` in `release-please-config.json`).

Write the title (and description) as the changelog line a user should read.

## Releasing

There's no manual version bump, tag, or upload:

1. Merge pull requests to `main` as usual. After each merge, release-please
   opens or updates a **`chore(main): release X.Y.Z`** pull request that bumps
   the version in `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` and prepends the new section to
   `CHANGELOG.md`.
2. When you're ready to release, review that PR (edit its changelog section or
   description if needed; the description becomes the GitHub release notes)
   and merge it. Edits are overwritten if anything else merges to `main` first.
3. The merge tags `vX.Y.Z`, creates the GitHub release, and attaches the
   built sdist and wheel to it.

To force a specific version, merge a commit to `main` whose body contains a
`Release-As: X.Y.Z` footer.
[%- else %]

### How titles drive releases

The titles decide the next version and the changelog entries:

| Title                                                              | Version bump | Changelog section        |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------ | ------------------------ |
| `feat!:` / `BREAKING CHANGE:`                                      | major        | ⚠ BREAKING CHANGES       |
| `feat:`                                                            | minor        | Features                 |
| `fix:`                                                             | patch        | Bug Fixes                |
| `perf:`                                                            | patch        | Performance Improvements |
| `revert:`                                                          | patch        | Reverts                  |
| `build:`, `chore:`, `ci:`, `docs:`, `refactor:`, `style:`, `test:` | none         | not listed               |

Write the title (and description) as the changelog line a user should read.

## Releasing

Releases are made by hand, in a pull request of their own:

1. Run `git fetch origin`, list the titles merged since the last release
   (`git log --oneline v<last>..origin/main`, or everything for the first
   release), and pick the bump from the table above.
2. Run `uv version --bump major|minor|patch`, which updates `pyproject.toml`
   and `uv.lock`.
3. Add a `## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD` section to the top of `CHANGELOG.md`
   with the titles under the sections above.
4. Open a pull request titled `chore: release X.Y.Z`, and merge it.
5. Fetch the merge commit, then tag it and push the tag:
   `git fetch origin && git tag vX.Y.Z origin/main && git push origin vX.Y.Z`.
[%- endif %]
